# Extra known failures when the guest runs under QEMU TCG on a slow host
# (hosted CI runners), merged with qemutest.xfail via a second --xfail.
# All of these pass on KVM and on a fast TCG host.
#
# Timer-driven tests (alarm/setitimer/signal timing).  On a runner where
# TCG needs about five minutes just to reach login:, they print their TAP
# plan and never see their alarm fire; the guest itself stays responsive
# (37 once wedged the console and cost a reboot) and the next test runs.
# Bounded to two minutes so a stall costs little.  Roadmap: T1, tick-based
# timers under a host that steals most of the guest's time.
1:120 flaky
5:120 flaky
37:120 flaky
41:120 flaky
76:120 flaky

# File-system tests that loop over thousands of calls; on the runner they
# had not finished after the default 20 minutes (CI run 9).  Ten minutes
# lets them pass on a good day without eating the shard's budget.  25 is
# already flaky on KVM (qemutest.xfail); this entry only adds the bound.
25:600 flaky
27:600 flaky
28:600 flaky
32:600 flaky

# Pure syscall throughput: two processes doing 5000 x 1000 lseek()s.  59 s
# on KVM, 565 s under TCG on a desktop, past the default 20 minutes on the
# hosted runners once the clock ran at the right rate (PR #362).  Half an
# hour lets it finish there without holding the shard hostage.
70:1800 flaky

# Storage hierarchy: 450 s on the hosted runners under TCG when it works.
# Overrides the KVM-sized bound in qemutest.xfail (the later list wins).
71:1200 flaky
